As NVMe bandwidth increases and device latency falls, the software overhead of the user-kernel storage path has become a first-order bottleneck for fine-grained I/O. Although operating systems provide scatter-gather interfaces such as preadv and pwritev to amortise this cost, integrating these interfaces into mature storage engines typically requires invasive refactoring across buffer management, logging, and caching layers. As a result, logically sequential writes are often emitted as long streams of scalar system calls. We present IOOpt, an LLVM link-time optimisation pass that transparently coalesces fragmented scalar I/O into vectored operations at compile time. IOOpt combines whole-program visibility with Scalar Evolution-based offset reasoning, root-pointer analysis for buffer provenance, and conservative barrier detection to preserve program order and durability boundaries. When vectored I/O is not profitable, IOOpt can instead synthesise bounded shadow-buffer writes for small batches. Across five representative benchmarks; database WAL, HTTP response generation, media streaming, cross-translation-unit logging, and MPI-I/O; IOOpt reduces execution time by 38 - 70% and achieves speed-ups of over 3x without source-code modification. These results suggest that compile and link-time I/O coalescing is a practical mechanism for recovering performance lost to storage-software abstraction.